WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.320
You have never met most of the viruses that will ever infect you.

2
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.600
Yet your body already holds B cells whose receptors can bind them.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.140
How can a defense be ready for a molecule before it exists in your world?

4
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.220
The answer is a rule called clonal selection.

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:25.103
In vertebrates, adaptive immunity is carried by lymphocytes, a kind of white blood

6
00:00:25.103 --> 00:00:30.543
cell. Each lymphocyte makes receptors of one specificity, and it makes them before

7
00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:31.743
it ever meets antigen.

8
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.203
An antigen is the molecule a receptor binds.

9
00:00:36.143 --> 00:00:40.663
When antigen arrives, it binds only the few cells whose receptors already fit.

10
00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:45.903
Those cells divide into a clone, a population descended from that one cell.

11
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:52.377
How does a body make millions of receptor shapes from a human genome of about 20,000

12
00:00:52.377 --> 00:00:53.927
protein-coding genes?

13
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:58.967
The part of an antibody that binds antigen is not encoded as one piece.

14
00:00:59.887 --> 00:01:05.227
In each developing lymphocyte, the cell joins one V, one D, and one J segment for a

15
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:08.567
heavy chain. This is called somatic recombination.

16
00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:12.027
At each joint, nucleotides are added or removed.

17
00:01:12.787 --> 00:01:14.227
That is junctional variation.

18
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:18.807
Count the possibilities, using illustrative human numbers.

19
00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:25.267
40 V x 23 D x 6 J gives 5,520 heavy chains.

20
00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:27.527
Light chains come in two kinds.

21
00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:32.147
Kappa gives 200 combinations, and lambda gives 120.

22
00:01:32.987 --> 00:01:34.947
That makes 320 light chains.

23
00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:38.647
Each B cell pairs one heavy chain with one light chain.

24
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:44.367
Multiplying gives about 1.8 million receptors, if every combination works.

25
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.487
That is from fewer than 150 segments.

26
00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:51.567
No antigen was needed to make any of these shapes.

27
00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:56.927
After a first response, which takes days to build, most of the expanded cells die.

28
00:01:57.707 --> 00:02:00.507
But memory B cells and memory T cells remain.

29
00:02:01.427 --> 00:02:05.047
A second encounter starts from a larger, already selected population.

30
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.627
So the secondary response is faster and larger.

31
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.490
Some earlier theories held that antigen acts as a template, folding a general

32
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.290
antibody into a matching shape.

33
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.130
Clonal selection replaced that idea.

34
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.370
Receptors are made first, by recombination.

35
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.650
Antigen finds the cells that already fit, and they become a clone.

36
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.230
Keep this rule. The antigen chooses.

37
00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.130
It does not shape.
